# Line endings. Every text file is LF in the repository and LF in the working
# tree, on every platform. `text=auto` still lets git detect binary content and
# leave it alone; the explicit `-text` entries below are belt-and-braces for
# formats where a single converted byte corrupts the file.
#
# Added 2026-08-21. Before it the tree was almost all LF already — 8 files of
# 1533 were CRLF — but nothing enforced that, and an editor writing CRLF was
# invisible until a diff showed a whole file rewritten. Measure with
# `git ls-files --eol`, which is the authoritative tool here; a grep for a
# carriage return is easy to get wrong.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary: never convert, never diff as text.
*.phar  -text
*.png   -text
*.jpg   -text
*.ico   -text
*.eot   -text
*.otf   -text
*.ttf   -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text

# THE MIGRATION LEDGER FREEZES THESE FOUR FILES' BYTES.
#
# `sch_migration` records `sha256sum` of each applied migration and
# tools/migrate.sh refuses to run when a recorded hash no longer matches what is
# on disk — it is called by the deploy at both the pre and post phase, so a
# changed byte here does not fail a test, it fails the next deploy. These four
# are the only migrations still CRLF, and normalising them would change all four
# hashes. `-text` is what makes git incapable of converting them.
#
# The cost is that git shows them as binary rather than diffing them, which for a
# file whose bytes are frozen by design is closer to the truth than a diff view.
# The other 74 migrations are already LF and stay diffable; a new migration is
# written LF and hashed LF, so this never grows.
database/db0.0.sql -text
database/db0.3.sql -text
database/db0.4.sql -text
database/db0.5.sql -text
